WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.160
The woman fell to the ground,&nbsp;
clutching her ankle in pain. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:09.300
had crunched badly as she'd fallen,&nbsp;
and now, as she looked down at it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:14.280
she could see that it was bent totally out of&nbsp;
shape. The heavy metal collar choking her neck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:19.320
made it difficult for her to catch her breath&nbsp;
as she tried her best not to hyperventilate.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:24.780
The rain was hammering down so ferociously&nbsp;
that she could barely see more than 20 feet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:30.420
in any direction. But she didn't need to see&nbsp;
far to know how deadly of a predicament she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:35.880
had found herself in. She cried out at the top&nbsp;
of her voice for someone to come and find her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:41.220
anyone, but the only sound that filled&nbsp;
her ears was the wild howling of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:45.660
wind and the steady hammering of&nbsp;
water into the bog all around her.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:52.980
She would get hypothermia and die. No, wait, she&nbsp;
couldn’t, could she? She knew she couldn't die,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:58.380
but she also knew that she was still capable&nbsp;
of suffering. Just how bad would it feel to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:02.640
be in the clutches of hypothermia with&nbsp;
a broken ankle and know that no matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:07.500
how bad the pain and suffering got, she&nbsp;
would never feel the release of death?

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:12.000
She closed her eyes, trying her best&nbsp;
not to think of the worst possibilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.860
just as the sound of gunfire&nbsp;
erupted in the nearby buffer.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:21.660
She cried out again, holding both hands up in&nbsp;
the air, doing the best she could around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:27.300
heavy metal collar, but no one shouted out in&nbsp;
reply. There was a pause in the gunfire. Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:31.440
they'd heard her. Maybe they were trying to&nbsp;
figure out a way to come down and rescue her.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:32.280
CRACK!

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.880
A bullet split open a rotten&nbsp;
tree trunk just a few feet to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:39.480
her right. The woman threw her hands&nbsp;
over her head and cowered in the dirt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:43.380
feeling the metal of her collar digging&nbsp;
into her skin tighter than ever.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:47.460
Please don’t let it happen again,&nbsp;
she prayed silently into the dirt.

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:54.540
Encased in the collar around her neck were 4.5kg&nbsp;
of plastic explosives, each with a fragmentation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.540 --> 00:02:00.360
layer pointed inwards towards her neck and head.&nbsp;
With just the push of a button, they could...

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.120
She didn't want to think about it. All she knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.420
was that she couldn't let&nbsp;
that happen to her again.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:12.180
Looking through eyes bloodied with tears and&nbsp;
rain, she saw lying in the dirt just a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:18.180
feet away from her that infernal machine&nbsp;
that was the sorry cause of all of this.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:18.540
*

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:22.860
The SCP Foundation has long stood&nbsp;
as a bastion of hope, information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:28.380
and security for the entirety of the human&nbsp;
race. I'm sure you are well aware of the number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:33.780
of world-destroying catastrophes that have been&nbsp;
averted by the hardworking researchers, agents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:39.060
and other members of the Foundation—all without&nbsp;
the general public having the slightest clue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:44.580
that something was amiss. With state-of-the-art&nbsp;
holding cells all over the globe and the sharpest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:50.160
minds humanity has ever produced working around&nbsp;
the clock to ensure the protection of humanity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:55.080
it is hard to imagine an entity that&nbsp;
should not be contained by such a group.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:59.796
And yet one such entity does exist—SCP-001.

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:06.660
You may have come across the O5 Council by this&nbsp;
point. While the SCP Foundation operates beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:13.020
the world’s jurisdiction, the O5 Council operates&nbsp;
beyond the Foundation's jurisdiction. The rules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:17.280
methods, and protocols that are the&nbsp;
cornerstone of securing, containing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.180
and protecting countless entities&nbsp;
around the world sometimes cannot be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:25.920
applied. Certain entities require us&nbsp;
to temporarily abandon our humanity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:32.280
abandon our sense of order, and step&nbsp;
briefly into chaos… for the greater good.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:39.960
I'm telling you all of this because SCP-001 is not&nbsp;
held in a containment cell. There are no keypads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:45.900
locked doors, observation windows, or&nbsp;
health and safety forms. SCP-001 is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:51.420
not confined to a specific territory or&nbsp;
even a specific country. For some SCPs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:56.580
this is a practical necessity—serpents that are&nbsp;
hundreds of kilometers long swimming through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:04.260
the depths of the ocean, for example. But for&nbsp;
SCP-001, it serves a more… psychological purpose.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:04.620
*

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:09.120
The Scottish Highlands are the most remote&nbsp;
part of the United Kingdom. Out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:15.600
you can walk for miles and miles without seeing a&nbsp;
single soul. Open countryside, mountains, lochs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:21.120
and forests surround you in all directions. The&nbsp;
weather is harsh and unrelenting, the walking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:26.760
even more so. On a regular basis, walkers fall&nbsp;
and break their legs, but without phone service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:32.220
or anyone else nearby to come and help, they can&nbsp;
quickly disappear into the wilderness forever.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:38.580
The peat bogs of Scotland used to see human&nbsp;
sacrifices in early settlements. Afraid of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:43.680
ghosts and spirits that haunted the bogs, people&nbsp;
would throw their family members into the deepest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:49.740
parts and watch them drown, hoping that whatever&nbsp;
was lurking beneath wouldn't come and find them.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:53.280
So as the group of soldiers crested&nbsp;
the top of the mountain and looked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:58.500
down beneath them to the eerie peat bogs&nbsp;
obscured by mist and constant rainfall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:04.080
you would forgive a shiver running up&nbsp;
their spines. But, of course, it didn't.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:10.260
That was because this group of soldiers were quite&nbsp;
unlike any others that humanity has ever produced.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:17.100
All were nameless. None of them existed on any&nbsp;
government databases, on any Foundation databases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:24.000
or even on the databases of the O5 Council itself.&nbsp;
Their individual backgrounds, nationalities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:28.980
and families were totally unknown to&nbsp;
everyone other than themselves. Some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:34.560
had been tortured for years, others had been the&nbsp;
torturers. The thing that united them, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:41.520
was their inhuman ruthlessness—a squad of eight&nbsp;
soldiers utterly devoid of any sense of empathy.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:46.620
What could they possibly have to be&nbsp;
afraid of in the peat bogs when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:49.440
themselves were the evil ghosts walking through?

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:54.840
But then, one person was afraid—a&nbsp;
shiver ran up her spine as she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:58.860
looked over the edge and down into&nbsp;
the murky black and green below.

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:00.720
SCP-001-1.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:07.140
Around her neck was the bomb collar. Each&nbsp;
of the eight soldiers surrounding her had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:11.280
a button mounted on a watch on the&nbsp;
back of their wrists. At any moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.660
any one of them could hit the button, and&nbsp;
all four and a half kilograms of plastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:20.520
explosives would go off, sending&nbsp;
her on an express trip to oblivion.

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:26.220
Aside from the collar, she wore a plain white&nbsp;
dress made of cotton. It was muddy and torn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:31.080
apart at the hem from days of walking through the&nbsp;
Scottish wilderness. When she had first arrived,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:36.360
she had begged those around her to supply her with&nbsp;
some warm clothes, something practical that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:42.480
keep her comfortable and stave off any illness.&nbsp;
Her requests, however, had not been acknowledged.

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:50.220
Clutched in her trembling, outstretched&nbsp;
hands was the Machine. SCP-001-A. From&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:54.780
its external appearance, you would think&nbsp;
it was nothing more than a wooden box—a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:58.860
perfect cuboid made from glossy&nbsp;
dark wood. There were no symbols,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:03.420
no seams, no latches—nothing to&nbsp;
indicate any method of operation.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:07.920
When the woman and the machine had first been&nbsp;
delivered into the hands of the O5 Council,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:12.780
the researchers had spent weeks and millions&nbsp;
of dollars trying to activate the Machine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:17.400
Their best scientists had scanned for&nbsp;
every possible form of radiation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:21.720
tried every method they could conceive&nbsp;
to stimulate the box into opening.

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:26.940
The ultimate failed attempt involved traveling&nbsp;
to North Korea, where they negotiated placing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:31.620
the box 20 centimeters beneath a small&nbsp;
nuclear warhead in return for granting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:35.940
the dictatorship key information&nbsp;
on how to construct such a weapon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:41.040
As we have established, the O5 Council&nbsp;
operates beyond any kind of jurisdiction.

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:46.740
Yet, at the bottom of the irradiated&nbsp;
crater, sat a perfectly intact wooden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:51.300
box that was cool to the touch and&nbsp;
showed no signs of radioactivity.

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:58.500
Only one person could interact with the box and&nbsp;
unearth the secrets that were inside—SCP-001-1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:02.400
the woman who stood trembling&nbsp;
on the side of the mountain.

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:07.560
A gun jabbed her in the back, forcing her to&nbsp;
continue moving. She asked the soldiers around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:13.380
her how much longer they had to walk that day,&nbsp;
but none of them replied. They never did. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:18.660
six years that she had been held hostage by&nbsp;
this tiny militia, she had never once heard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:24.960
any of them say a word, not even to each other.&nbsp;
Perhaps it was this telepathic understanding that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:30.360
seemed to run between them that unnerved her the&nbsp;
most. Despite having never spoken to each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:35.160
each soldier seemed to understand the&nbsp;
others intimately, and she had no doubt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:39.600
that any one of the eight would press the&nbsp;
button on their wrist at a moment's notice.

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:45.900
Sadly for her, she knew this from experience.&nbsp;
The incident had happened four years ago now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:51.000
as the group was traveling through Patagonia.&nbsp;
It was a day almost identical to the one she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:56.040
was having now. They had been transported by&nbsp;
a helicopter flown by one of the eight into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.040 --> 00:09:00.840
the middle of the wilderness. There, they had&nbsp;
marched for days without saying a single word.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:06.720
Exhaustion had overtaken her legs, and she&nbsp;
stumbled to the ground. Unfortunately for her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:11.340
this happened slightly too close to&nbsp;
the female soldier in front of her.

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:16.320
Her knees hadn't even hit the ground before the&nbsp;
blast went off. The woman didn't remember it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:21.780
of course—how could she? In an instant, her&nbsp;
mind had been utterly destroyed. What she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:26.040
did remember was the next 18 months&nbsp;
as her body slowly healed itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:31.140
one brain cell at a time. It wasn't so&nbsp;
much like waking up from a nightmare;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:37.560
it was more constructing a nightmare slowly,&nbsp;
alongside your consciousness as neuron by neuron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:45.060
your brain reformed itself. Each individual cell&nbsp;
screaming in terror at what had happened to it.

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:49.620
They had her marching again before she&nbsp;
was fit to move. Her motor controls had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:53.520
been all over the place. She had fallen&nbsp;
over regularly, and the terror of having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:57.240
one of the soldiers push the button&nbsp;
again engulfed her with every movement.

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:01.020
And yet, perhaps the most&nbsp;
incredible thing about SCP-001-1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:05.700
was the fact that if you had asked her if&nbsp;
she should have been held in this kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:09.660
containment, she would immediately&nbsp;
have agreed without batting an eye.

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:14.940
The only person capable of opening the box,&nbsp;
she recognized how dangerous her existence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:21.180
was. Only she had seen into the mysteries of&nbsp;
the box, only she had seen the horrors laid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:26.700
inside of it, and so only she could fully&nbsp;
understand the gravity of their situation.

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:31.980
They kept her on the move in order to keep the&nbsp;
world safe. Had she been held in a containment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:37.800
cell, she would have posed too great a risk. Out&nbsp;
here in the wilderness, the entire planet was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:43.260
her containment cell—hidden in the middle&nbsp;
of humanity's biggest haystack. No one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:48.720
not even the O5 Council’s central command, knew&nbsp;
her location. The only people who were aware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:54.360
of it at any given time were herself and the&nbsp;
eight soldiers surrounding her with guns drawn.

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:59.160
So you can imagine her horror when, out of&nbsp;
the sheets of rain, appeared the figure of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:05.700
a person carrying a rifle. The gunfire broke&nbsp;
out before SCP-001-1 even had a chance to hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:10.560
the ground. Bullets whizzed through her hair and&nbsp;
cracked open the rocks all around her. The eight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:14.640
soldiers surrounding her dived for cover as&nbsp;
the figure in the rain slumped to the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:19.800
lifeless. One of the soldiers grabbed the woman by&nbsp;
her explosive collar and threw her behind a rock.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:25.560
Clasping her hands over her ears, she closed her&nbsp;
eyes and waited for the fight to be over. No one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:31.500
was shooting, until a second figure emerged from&nbsp;
the rain waving their arms wildly. Gunfire again.

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:35.160
She wasn't quite sure what had&nbsp;
happened, but all of a sudden,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:39.360
the woman was falling down the cliff. She&nbsp;
had just been trying to shift her position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:45.180
to get deeper into cover, but she clearly hadn't&nbsp;
noticed just how close to the edge she was.

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:49.800
Down and down and down, she fell&nbsp;
until, with a crack in her ankle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:55.560
she landed in the peat bog. Gunfire crackled on&nbsp;
the mountain above her, but the only thought that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.560 --> 00:12:00.960
filled the woman's mind was the terror that at&nbsp;
any moment, the explosive collar around her neck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:05.700
would be detonated as one of the soldiers&nbsp;
above her realized that she was missing.

00:12:05.700 --> 00:12:10.860
Seconds passed as the fear mounted in&nbsp;
her chest. With each passing moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.860 --> 00:12:17.100
the anxiety grew more and more crippling. She&nbsp;
had to know. She had to prepare herself if it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:21.240
was about to happen. She had to use&nbsp;
the machine and look into the future.

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:26.640
Dragging herself forward through the muck, the&nbsp;
woman snatched at the wooden box. It came alive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:32.400
at her touch. Different pieces shifted and opened&nbsp;
beneath her fingers like some kind of elaborate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:38.520
puzzle. No one had taught her how to use this&nbsp;
thing; it just happened. Her fingers would just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:45.540
dance across its surfaces, pushing and pulling,&nbsp;
opening and closing, twisting and turning, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:51.240
locking into place until all of a sudden, there&nbsp;
it was—the box was wide open in front of her.

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:55.500
Taking a deep breath and allowing the rain&nbsp;
to fall on her head for another brief moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:59.400
the woman leaned forward and stepped into the box.

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:04.140
On the trail above her, the gunfire stopped.&nbsp;
Without a word of communication, the soldiers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.140 --> 00:13:08.040
had deftly flanked the group of people who&nbsp;
had approached them. In less than a minute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:11.760
they neutralized each individual&nbsp;
that came their way. In unison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:15.840
the group of them walked up to the bodies,&nbsp;
turning them over to examine their faces.

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:21.420
They were nobodies, just a group of hikers lost&nbsp;
in the rain. What had looked like a gun turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:25.920
out to be nothing more than a walking pole.&nbsp;
Five of them in total, none of them older&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:30.600
than 24. Without words, the soldiers picked up&nbsp;
the bodies and threw them over their shoulders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:35.280
as they scrambled their way down the cliffs&nbsp;
to the woman in the machine beneath them.

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:40.200
Once they reached the bottom of the slope, they&nbsp;
discarded the five bodies carelessly into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:45.300
bog. Within a couple of hours, those five hikers&nbsp;
would have sunk to the bottom and begun the long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:50.940
process of being embalmed into the depths. Perhaps&nbsp;
someone would find them in a few thousand years'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:55.440
time as part of an archaeological dig.&nbsp;
Perhaps, but it was not their concern.

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:59.520
The eight soldiers surrounded the woman,&nbsp;
guns drawn, and stared at her coldly.

00:13:59.520 --> 00:14:04.560
“I used the machine,” she told them. “I used it&nbsp;
without your permission. I don't know what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:07.860
rules are, I don't know if we even have any&nbsp;
rules here, but I thought you should know.”

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:12.000
The soldiers continued to look at her&nbsp;
in silence. The box was closed now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:14.340
sitting back on her lap as it always was.

00:14:14.340 --> 00:14:19.560
“I… I tried to see my future. Anytime I've used&nbsp;
the box before, I've looked at the lives of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:24.540
others. I've seen economic crashes, climate&nbsp;
disasters, genocides, wars, love and life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:29.520
and death. I've done so at the hands of the O5&nbsp;
Council, done as they've told me, given them the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:33.660
information, and prevented the destruction of the&nbsp;
world. Never once have I looked at my own future.”

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:38.520
The soldiers lifted her to her feet and tried&nbsp;
to march her onwards, not listening to a word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:43.140
she was saying. Her broken ankle buckled&nbsp;
and screamed beneath her. She had to hop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:48.000
to keep up with them. What other choice did she&nbsp;
have? They would push the button if she didn't.

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:51.060
She didn’t know why she kept talking. But she did.

00:14:51.060 --> 00:14:55.680
“For the first time… the machine lied&nbsp;
to me. I saw that I was assassinated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.680 --> 00:14:59.640
in 1987 in Cuba. It was years&nbsp;
before I ever built the box,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:04.440
before any of this happened to me. I saw&nbsp;
in my future that I no longer existed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.440 --> 00:15:09.000
that the Machine no longer existed, but that&nbsp;
future was years ago, and none of it happened.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:13.320
The machine doesn’t lie. So why is it lying&nbsp;
to me now? Why can’t I see my own future?”

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:17.640
If any of the soldiers were paying&nbsp;
attention, they didn’t show it. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:22.620
just continued to march her into the rain,&nbsp;
as the bomb weighed heavily around her neck.

